g with this practical experience and the class will depend on the experience they have up to date with phonology, phonetics, morphology, syntax, semantics. Prerequisites: LING 301, LING 302, and one of the following courses: LING 304 or LING 307."},{"crn":"70579","code":"U3","type":"Lecture-Discussion","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Lecture-Discussion","days":["M","W"],"start":780,"end":860,"location":"1126 Literatures, Cultures, & Ling","instructors":["Talic, A"]}],"restrictions":["Restricted to Undergrad - Urbana-Champaign."],"note":"This is a hands-on (learn-by-doing) practical course in elicitation of linguistic data, and theoretical analysis of that data, from an unfamiliar language. Students will elicit, record, and transcribe data collected in group interviews. The class as a whole will work on a language archive: (i) recordings collected throughout the semester and their transcriptions, (ii) a dictionary with vocabulary translated into English, (iii) the final projects. This course provides advanced undergraduate students with the opportunity to integrate their previous training with this practical experience and the class will depend on the experience they have up to date with phonology, phonetics, morphology, syntax, semantics. Prerequisites: LING 301, LING 302, and one of the following courses: LING 304 or LING 307."}]},{"id":"LING 404","title":"Tutorials in Non-Western Lang","creditHours":"1 TO 5 hours.","sections":[{"crn":"36461","code":"HND","type":"Lecture-Discussion","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Lecture-Discussion","days":[],"start":null,"end":null,"location":"Location Pending","instructors":["Mishra, M"]}],"restrictions":[],"note":""},{"crn":"43005","code":"SWA","type":"Online","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Online","days":[],"start":null,"end":null,"location":"","instructors":["Toure, V"]}],"restrictions":[],"note":""}]},{"id":"LING 414","title":"Advanced Computational Linguistics","creditHours":"3 OR 4 hours.","sections":[{"crn":"77825","code":"G4","type":"Lecture-Discussion","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Lecture-Discussion","days":["T","R"],"start":750,"end":830,"location":"203 Transportation Building","instructors":["Maskharashvili, A"]}],"restrictions":["Restricted to Graduate - Urbana-Champaign."],"note":""},{"crn":"77824","code":"UG3","type":"Lecture-Discussion","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Lecture-Discussion","days":["T","R"],"start":750,"end":830,"location":"203 Transportation Building","instructors":["Maskharashvili, A"]}],"restrictions":["Restricted to Undergrad - Urbana-Champaign."],"note":""}]},{"id":"LING 418","title":"Language & Minorities in Europe","creditHours":"3 OR 4 hours.","sections":[{"crn":"50305","code":"1G","type":"Lecture-Discussion","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Lecture-Discussion","days":["T"],"start":960,"end":1040,"location":"321 Gregory Hall","instructors":["Derhemi, E"]},{"type":"Online","days":["R"],"start":960,"end":1040,"location":"","instructors":["Derhemi, E"]}],"restrictions":["Restricted to Graduate - Urbana-Champaign."],"note":"Language & Minorities in Europe. Credit: 3 or 4 hours. Survey of regional and immigrant minority language use, policies, and planning across Europe. Focus on political and social issues, such as language regimes, education, loss, and maintenance. Two immigrant languages, Turkish and Arabic/Berber, and four indigenous language families: Balto-Slavic with Estonian, Celtic, Romance with Basque, and Slavic with Hungarian. Taught in English. Same as GER 418, ITAL 418, LING 418, PS 418, SLAV 418, SPAN 418, and EURO 418. 3 undergraduate hours. 4 graduate hours."},{"crn":"50304","code":"1U","type":"Lecture-Discussion","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Lecture-Discussion","days":["T"],"start":960,"end":1040,"location":"321 Gregory Hall","instructors":["Derhemi, E"]},{"type":"Online","days":["R"],"start":960,"end":1040,"location":"","instructors":["Derhemi, E"]}],"restrictions":["Restricted to Undergrad - Urbana-Champaign."],"note":"Language & Minorities in Europe. Credit: 3 or 4 hours. Survey of regional and immigrant minority language use, policies, and planning across Europe. Focus on political and social issues, such as language regimes, education, loss, and maintenance. Two immigrant languages, Turkish and Arabic/Berber, and four indigenous language families: Balto-Slavic with Estonian, Celtic, Romance with Basque, and Slavic with Hungarian. Taught in English. Same as GER 418, ITAL 418, LING 418, PS 418, SLAV 418, SPAN 418, and EURO 418. 3 undergraduate hours. 4 graduate hours."}]},{"id":"LING 425","title":"Intro to Psycholinguistics","creditHours":"3 OR 4 hours.","sections":[{"crn":"46351","code":"A3","type":"Lecture-Discussion","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Lecture-Discussion","days":["M","W"],"start":870,"end":950,"location":"313 Gregory Hall","instructors":["Tachihara, K"]}],"restrictions":["Restricted to Undergrad - Urbana-Champaign."],"note":"Section for undergraduate students"},{"crn":"46352","code":"A4","type":"Lecture-Discussion","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Lecture-Discussion","days":["M","W"],"start":870,"end":950,"location":"313 Gregory Hall","instructors":["Tachihara, K"]}],"restrictions":["Restricted to Graduate - Urbana-Champaign."],"note":"Section for graduate students"}]},{"id":"LING 427","title":"Language and the Brain","creditHours":"3 OR 4 hours.","sections":[{"crn":"50795","code":"G4","type":"Lecture","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Lecture","days":["T","R"],"start":750,"end":830,"location":"3025 Campus Instructional Facility","instructors":["Pucilowski, A"]}],"restrictions":["Restricted to Graduate - Urbana-Champaign."],"note":"Graduates register for this section. How the human brain supports production and comprehension of language. Topics covered include: neuroanatomy of language; neuroimaging of language; language disorders; brain lateralization for language; bilingualism and the brain; sign language and the brain."},{"crn":"50794","code":"UG3","type":"Lecture","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Lecture","days":["T","R"],"start":750,"end":830,"location":"3025 Campus Instructional Facility","instructors":["Pucilowski, A"]}],"restrictions":["Restricted to Undergrad - Urbana-Champaign."],"note":"Undergraduates register for this section. How the human brain supports production and comprehension of language. Topics covered include: neuroanatomy of language; neuroimaging of language; language disorders; brain lateralization for language; bilingualism and the brain; sign language and the brain."}]},{"id":"LING 446","title":"Fundamentals for Speech Signal Processing and Analysis","creditHours":"3 OR 4 hours.","sections":[{"crn":"72395","code":"G4","type":"Lecture-Discussion","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Lecture-Discussion","days":["T","R"],"start":840,"end":920,"location":"G48 Literatures, Cultures, & Ling","instructors":["Tang, Y"]}],"restrictions":["Restricted to Graduate - Urbana-Champaign."],"note":"Provides an elementary introduction to concepts, principles and algorithms of digital signal processing. It focuses on computational implementations of contemporary methodologies in digital signal processing rather than underlying mathematical theories, and therefore requires students to have Python or MATLAB programming skills as prerequisite. This course comprises lectures and laboratory sessions, during which students are expected to produce their own computer code aided by ready-made programs to solve practical problems. 3 undergraduate hours. 4 graduate hours. Prerequisite: LING 402 or equivalent. Although not required, knowledge in algebra and trigonometry is an advantage."},{"crn":"72396","code":"U3","type":"Lecture-Discussion","partOfTerm":"1","meetings":[{"type":"Lecture-Discussion","days":["T","R"],"start":840,"end":920,"location":"G48 Literatures, Cultures, & Ling","instructors":["Tang, Y"]}],"restrictions":["Restricted to Undergrad - Urbana-Champaign."],"note"